import Mathlib

/-!
# Lattice Yang–Mills: a single-file Lean 4 development

This file collects a Lean 4 / Mathlib development around the lattice Yang–Mills mass-gap
programme: the W₁ Dobrushin comparison and the couplings it needs, elementary bounds on the Wilson
action, the one-loop running coupling, a no-go theorem for lattice gaps that are uniform in the
spacing, the Holley–Stroock perturbation lemma, finite combinatorics of gauge fixings and blocks,
the arithmetic of the sharp-quarter Dobrushin threshold for SU(2), and verified building blocks of
a Monte Carlo renormalisation-group (MCRG) code. It imports Mathlib only and contains no `sorry`,
no `axiom` declarations and no `native_decide`. It does not prove a mass gap for Yang–Mills: each
part states its own scope, and the inputs that remain open are named there.

## Contents

| part | section | mathematical content |
|---|---|---|
| 01 | `Part01_CouplingProgramme` | The coupling programme, in eight sub-parts: the arithmetic half of the W₁ Dobrushin comparison (column- and row-weighted forms); a discrepancy recurrence from minimality of a weighted coupling cost; Neumann-series and resolvent bounds, decay with a floor, finite propagation and the measure-theoretic coupling interface; G1 on a finite spin space; cost-minimising couplings on a compact metric spin space; G1 on a compact metric spin space from finite discretisations; gluing of finite couplings and kernels close to a Dobrushin kernel; reference kernels obtained by quantising the continuum kernels. |
| 02 | `Part02_WilsonAction` | The real part of the trace of a unitary `N × N` matrix is at most `N` in absolute value; bounds on the Wilson action and its oscillation; the Holley–Stroock factor tends to `0` as the number of plaquettes grows; the six oriented plaquette angles of a cube sum to zero, so plaquette variables are not independent in dimension `d ≥ 3`. |
| 03 | `Part03_RunningCoupling` | The one-loop running coupling `g²(μ) = 1 / (2 b₀ log (μ / Λ))`: asymptotic freedom, and every value of the effective coupling is taken at a physical scale that does not depend on the lattice spacing. |
| 04 | `Part04_SpectralNoGo` | From the spectral representation of temporal two-point functions: a family with a lattice gap uniform in the spacing cannot have renormalised two-point functions converging to the two-point function of a nonzero finite spectral measure supported in `[Δ, ∞)`, `Δ > 0`. |
| 05 | `Part05_LimitRatio` | Under a lattice gap `Δ_lat(a) ≥ m_a · a` with `m_a → m`, the limiting two-point functions satisfy `L(t₂) ≤ exp (-(m (t₂ - t₁))) L(t₁)`. |
| 06 | `Part06_HolleyStroock` | The Holley–Stroock perturbation lemma on a finite state space, for the Poincaré and the entropy (log-Sobolev) inequality, with the factor `e^{osc V}`. |
| 07 | `Part07_CumulantCounterexample` | Bounded nonnegative local terms do not give a bound on the variance of the action per unit volume that is uniform in the volume. |
| 08 | `Part08_BlockGauge` | The block-rooted axial gauge on blocks of side two in four dimensions: tree and active-link counts, connectivity, and the twelve invisible classes. |
| 09 | `Part09_GaugeFix` | A gauge fixing of `ℤ⁴`, read modulo 2, with exactly one fixed link in every plaquette; every remaining link has `12` remaining partners in its six plaquettes. |
| 10 | `Part10_CubeOpen` | The finite arithmetic behind the stability of the cube condition under finite-range perturbations of the action, and the small-field constraint on the cube side. |
| 11 | `Part11_QxCoefficients` | The transverse half of the sharp quarter: every power-series coefficient of `D(k) = (8 + 2k²) I₁(k)² - k² I₀(k)² - k I₁(2k)` is nonnegative. |
| 12 | `Part12_Thresholds` | The Dobrushin threshold for SU(2) at the sharp quarter: Dobrushin row sums at most `6(d-1)` times the absolute value of `β`, the range `β_W < 2/9` at `d = 4`, and the positivity of the resulting gap. |
| 13 | `Part13_ZonalBound` | The zonal half of the sharp quarter: a strict subsolution of the Riccati equation for `I₂/I₁`, and the algebra showing that the zonal bound is strictly below `1/4` for `9k² ≤ 88`. |
| 14 | `Part14_ForestDobrushinProofs` | Standalone copies, with proofs, of the four theorems of sub-part 1 of Part 01, followed by `#print axioms` for each copy. |
| 15 | `Part15_ForestDobrushinMatch` | The copies in Part 14 have the same types as the declarations of sub-part 1 of Part 01. |
| 16 | `Part16_SharpBlockData` | Rational bounds, from outward-rounded interval enclosures, for the eigenvalues of the Hessian, the third derivative and the gradient of `log Z₀`, used by the sharpened plaquette-block constant for SU(2). |
| 17 | `Part17_SharpBlockCheck` | Exact rational arithmetic decided in the kernel: the block constant satisfies `α(0.351) ≤ 0.99997 < 1` (with further values at `0.345` and `1/3`), and the check fails at `0.352`. |
| 18 | `Part18_MCRG_qmul_assoc` | Namespace `YMMCRG`: quaternion algebra for SU(2) over a commutative ring (associativity, conjugation, norm); heat-bath and over-relaxation update identities; lattice gauge fields, holonomies and the gauge invariance of Wilson loops and staples; 2 × 2 blocking and APE smearing, also on the torus; independence of a checkerboard update pass from its order; tables of closed loops (plaquette, rectangle, chair, twist) and forest-gauge counts; Philox-4×32-10 known answers and an exact conversion to the open unit interval; a closed form for the terms of a series for `log Z₁`. Namespace `YMCert`: exact sum-of-squares certificates that the sixteen 8 × 8 vertex forms of a site-plus-plaquette certificate for the forest-gauge Hessian, and some further small quadratic forms, are positive definite. |

## Conventions and labels

* The top-level parts are numbered 01–18. Part 01 is split into sub-parts 1–8, and the comments
  inside Part 01 call them "Part 1" … "Part 8".
* **G1** is the formalisation of Lemma `comparison` of the forest-gauge paper (the W₁ Dobrushin
  comparison). Its arithmetic half is sub-part 1 of Part 01. The other, measure-theoretic half is
  the existence of a coupling of the two laws that satisfies the discrepancy recurrence.
* **Route 1** obtains G1 on a compact spin space as a limit of finite discretisations, without a
  measurable selection of couplings.
* **The discretisation step** is the input that route 1 still needs for the SU(2) Wilson heat
  baths: a Dobrushin bound for the discretised kernels, with an error that tends to zero uniformly
  in the boundary configuration, and weak convergence of the discretised Gibbs laws. It is open.
* **(D)** is the cube estimate `D_c(n, α)`, and **(E)** the existence of a nontrivial continuum
  limit satisfying the Osterwalder–Schrader axioms with a gap in physical units. Both are open, and
  neither is addressed here.
* "The forest-gauge paper" and "the accompanying notes" are the mathematical write-ups that some
  parts formalise. They are not needed to read the Lean statements.

## How this file was assembled

Each part was a separate Lean module. The modules were copied in dependency order, so that a part
uses only Mathlib and the parts above it. Their `import` lines were removed, since this file
imports Mathlib once, and each was wrapped in `section PartNN_<name> … end PartNN_<name>` so that
its `open` commands and variables stay local. Statements and proofs are unchanged. Comments that
pointed to the original files, their locations or their history were rewritten, and one namespace
and sixteen theorem names in Part 18 were renamed. Part 18 holds only the first part of its source
module; the rest of that module was a copy of Part 01, identical declaration by declaration.
Earlier drafts, duplicate copies of these modules and audit scripts are not included.

The file was checked with Lean v4.33.0 and Mathlib; the exact toolchain and Mathlib revision are
pinned in the Lake project files. Some kernel-decided computations are memory-intensive, so compile
it with a generous memory limit, for example `lake env lean -M24576 YangMills.lean`. The
`#print axioms` commands in Part 14 print information messages listing the axioms used.
-/

